.he "test (1)                    10/28/26                    test (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
test, [
- condition evaluation
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
test
expression
.br
.bd
[
expression
]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Test evaluates an expression and returns a zero exit status if it is
true, one if it is false, and a value greater than one if the expression
is malformed.  With no expression at all it is false.  Bracket is the
same program under a second name and requires the closing bracket as its
last argument.
.sp
The file primaries are:
.sp
-b file
.br
true if file exists and is a block special file.
.sp
-c file
.br
true if file exists and is a character special file.
.sp
-d file
.br
true if file exists and is a directory.
.sp
-e file
.br
true if file exists, whatever its type.
.sp
-f file
.br
true if file exists and is a regular file.
.sp
-g file
.br
true if file exists and its set group id flag is set.
.sp
-h file
.br
true if file exists and is a symbolic link.  Micronix has no symbolic
links, so this is always false.
.sp
-k file
.br
true if file exists and its sticky bit is set.  Micronix has no sticky
bit, so this is always false.
.sp
-p file
.br
true if file is a named pipe.  Micronix has no named pipes, so this is
always false.
.sp
-r file
.br
true if file exists and is readable.
.sp
-s file
.br
true if file exists and has a size greater than zero.
.sp
-t [fd]
.br
true if the descriptor, one if it is omitted, is associated with a
terminal.
.sp
-u file
.br
true if file exists and its set user id flag is set.
.sp
-w file
.br
true if file exists and is writable.
.sp
-x file
.br
true if file exists and is executable, or, if it is a directory, that
it can be searched.
.sp
For -r, -w and -x, a file the caller owns is tested on the owner's bits
and anything else on the other bits.  There is no group membership list
in this system, so the group bits are not consulted.
.sp
The string and integer primaries are:
.sp
-n string
.br
true if the length of string is nonzero.
.sp
-z string
.br
true if the length of string is zero.
.sp
string
.br
true if string is not the null string.
.sp
s1 = s2
.br
true if the strings s1 and s2 are identical.
.sp
s1 != s2
.br
true if the strings s1 and s2 are not identical.
.sp
n1 -eq n2
.br
true if the integers n1 and n2 are algebraically equal.
.sp
n1 -ne n2
.br
true if the integers n1 and n2 are not algebraically equal.
.sp
n1 -gt n2
.br
true if n1 is algebraically greater than n2.
.sp
n1 -ge n2
.br
true if n1 is algebraically greater than or equal to n2.
.sp
n1 -lt n2
.br
true if n1 is algebraically less than n2.
.sp
n1 -le n2
.br
true if n1 is algebraically less than or equal to n2.
.sp
Integers are read as 32-bit values.
.sp
The primaries are combined with:
.sp
! expression
.br
true if expression is false.
.sp
expression1 -a expression2
.br
true if both expressions are true.
.sp
expression1 -o expression2
.br
true if either expression is true.
.sp
( expression )
.br
true if expression is true.
.sp
-a binds more tightly than -o.  The operators are separate arguments,
so the shell must see them as words: quote the parentheses and the
exclamation mark.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
RETURN VALUES
.in +5
.br
0 if the expression is true, 1 if it is false or there is no expression,
and 2 if the expression is malformed or an operand is not an integer.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
GRAMMAR
.in +5
.br
The test grammar is inherently ambiguous.  The four cases the IEEE Std
1003.2 (POSIX) standard specifies are evaluated by its rules - a single
argument as a nonempty string, "! arg" as its negation, "arg1 op arg2"
as one comparison, and "! arg1 op arg2" as the negation of one - and
everything else is parsed by operator precedence.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLES
.in +5
.br
if test -f /etc/passwd -a -r /etc/passwd
.br
then echo readable
.br
fi
.sp
if [ -d /tmp ]
.br
then echo yes
.br
fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Test and bracket are two copies of one binary.  Micronix has no
symbolic links, and a hard link does not survive a tar(1) made here,
so the second name is installed as a copy.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sh (1), bsh (1), find (1)
